The build passed. There's only one test class in the project (`AcceptanceTest`, plus its non-test base/helpers), and the full suite is green.

## Summary

I implemented membership level 4 gated on tenure of more than 365 days.

**`Owner.java`** — added a domain method that expresses tenure as elapsed days since registration (returns 0 when the registration date is absent or hasn't yet elapsed), reusing the entity's existing `registrationDate` field:
```java
public long tenureInDays(LocalDate asOf) {
    if (this.registrationDate == null || asOf == null) {
        return 0;
    }
    return Math.max(0, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(this.registrationDate, asOf));
}
```

**`MembershipLevelEvaluator.java`** — added a fourth qualifying attribute (+1 when `tenureInDays > 365`), raised the cap `MAX_LEVEL` from 3 to 4, and injected a `Clock` (defaulting to system zone) so "today" stays testable and deterministic:
```java
if (hasQualifyingTenure(owner)) {
    level++;
}
...
private boolean hasQualifyingTenure(Owner owner) {
    return owner.tenureInDays(LocalDate.now(clock)) > TENURE_THRESHOLD_DAYS;
}
```

**Why a new owner never exceeds level 3:** a newly created owner's `registrationDate` defaults to the current date, so its tenure is 0 (≤ 365), leaving only the email and no-namesake attributes — max level 3. Level 4 becomes reachable only once tenure passes the threshold. This keeps each responsibility in its place: tenure math lives on the `Owner` domain object (mirroring the existing `AgeBand` date logic), and the level policy stays in the evaluator.

The acceptance test (`coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3`) and the full suite pass: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` — BUILD SUCCESS.

